# Response headers for the deployed site. Cloudflare reads this file out of the
# deploy bundle; it is never served to a browser.
#
# Note what is NOT here: an `immutable` cache on /assets/*. The media filenames
# carry no content hash (agent-session.mp4 keeps its name across re-records), so
# a year-long cache would strand visitors on a stale capture. An hour, with a
# week of stale-while-revalidate, keeps the edge doing the work without that.
#
# Two gotchas this file has already been bitten by:
#   - Rules ACCUMULATE. A second block matching the same path appends its
#     headers rather than replacing them, which on Content-Type yields a single
#     illegal `a, b` value. Never let two blocks set the same header on one path.
#   - There is no include mechanism, so the Link block below is repeated per
#     path on purpose. The Markdown twins need it as much as the pages do —
#     worker.js builds its response from the .md asset's headers, so whatever
#     is missing here is missing from every negotiated response too.
#   - hreflang Link targets must be FULLY QUALIFIED. RFC 8288 resolves a
#     relative target against the request URI, so `</zh/>` is perfectly legal
#     and works for every other rel type here — but Google ignores relative
#     hreflang, which makes the annotation silently worthless rather than
#     visibly broken. Measured against `wrangler dev`: a relative target ships
#     verbatim, so production really was serving `</zh/>` — while an absolute
#     one is re-serialized against whatever host answered the request (the dev
#     origin locally, weftcut.com deployed). Naming the origin here therefore
#     costs preview deploys nothing. Every other target stays relative only
#     because no rule makes it be absolute, not because it couldn't be.
#     build-dist.mjs enforces the hreflang half.
#     The two page blocks declare the whole set — self and x-default included —
#     to match index.html and sitemap.xml exactly. The .md blocks deliberately
#     declare only the other language: they canonicalise to their HTML twin two
#     lines above, so a self-reference there would contradict the canonical
#     sitting beside it. For them hreflang is a hint for agents, not an index
#     signal.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

# --- agent discovery ---------------------------------------------------------
# What an agent landing on the homepage can follow without guessing at URLs
# (RFC 8288). Everything here is a real file in the deploy bundle or a real page
# on GitHub — a Link to a 404 is worse than no Link at all.
#
# `alternate` + type=text/markdown is the static half of the deal worker.js
# makes: agents that negotiate with `Accept: text/markdown` get Markdown at this
# same URL, and agents that would rather just fetch a path have one to fetch.
/
  Link: </.well-known/api-catalog>; rel="api-catalog"; type="application/linkset+json"
  Link: </index.md>; rel="alternate"; type="text/markdown"
  Link: </llms.txt>; rel="describedby"; type="text/plain"; title="WeftCut in brief, for language models"
  Link: </.well-known/agent-skills/index.json>; rel="agent-skills"; type="application/json"
  Link: </.well-known/mcp/server-card.json>; rel="service-desc"; type="application/json"; title="WeftCut MCP server card"
  Link: <https://github.com/UncleChair/WeftCut/tree/main/docs>; rel="service-doc"; type="text/html"
  Link: <https://weftcut.com/>; rel="canonical"
  Link: <https://weftcut.com/>; rel="alternate"; hreflang="en"
  Link: <https://weftcut.com/zh/>; rel="alternate"; hreflang="zh-Hans"
  Link: <https://weftcut.com/>; rel="alternate"; hreflang="x-default"
  Link: <https://github.com/UncleChair/WeftCut/blob/main/LICENSE>; rel="license"; title="MIT"
  Vary: Accept

